################  experiment/exp8_limitations.py  ################

==========================================================================
1. The spectrum: undirected vs directed
==========================================================================
  ca-HepPh       (UNDIRECTED) n= 12,008  38 eigenvalues sampled
                   max |Im lambda| = 2.838e-14
  web-Stanford   (DIRECTED  ) n=281,903  59 eigenvalues sampled
                   max |Im lambda| = 9.900e-01

  The directed graph's spectrum is roughly 3e+13 times further
  off the real axis. That single difference is the whole story.

==========================================================================
2. What a complex spectrum does to the augmented matrix
==========================================================================
  ca-HepPh       beta = d^2/4 = 0.2450,  sqrt(beta) = 0.4950
                 strongest rival |mu| = 0.4950   |mu(lambda_1)| = 0.5705
                 rivals that OVERTAKE the dominant mode: 0 of 38   -> converges
  web-Stanford   beta = d^2/4 = 0.2450,  sqrt(beta) = 0.4950
                 strongest rival |mu| = 1.1950   |mu(lambda_1)| = 0.5705
                 rivals that OVERTAKE the dominant mode: 55 of 59   -> DIVERGES
                 worst: lambda = -0.0000+0.9900i, |lambda| = 0.9900, arg = +90.0 deg, |mu| = 1.1950
                 |mu| > 1, so this mode GROWS: the iteration does not
                 merely lose the race, it diverges outright.
                 What makes a mode damaging is its ANGLE, not its size:
                 this one sits 90 degrees off the real axis, which is
                 the worst case the disk |lambda| <= d allows.

==========================================================================
3. Convergence rate on web-Stanford
==========================================================================
